WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.887
Destination control is central to HTTP client security.

2
00:00:02.887 --> 00:00:07.578
The URL in code or configuration is not always the final network destination.

3
00:00:07.578 --> 00:00:10.826
Redirects can change the scheme, host, port, or path.

4
00:00:10.826 --> 00:00:13.713
Proxy settings can route traffic through another component.

5
00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:17.682
DNS resolution can map a name to different addresses over time.

6
00:00:17.682 --> 00:00:20.930
Each layer should be considered in the client policy.

7
00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.734
Redirect behavior should be explicit.

8
00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:25.621
Some clients should not follow redirects at all.

9
00:00:25.621 --> 00:00:31.395
Others may allow a small number of redirects within the same host or approved service boundary.

10
00:00:31.395 --> 00:00:37.890
Credential forwarding across redirects needs careful review because an authorization header, cookie, client certificate context, or sensitive request

11
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.499
body may no longer be appropriate after the destination changes.

12
00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:43.664
Proxy configuration is also security-relevant.

13
00:00:43.664 --> 00:00:47.994
A proxy may be required for enterprise egress, inspection, or network policy.

14
00:00:47.994 --> 00:00:51.963
It may also be supplied through environment settings or deployment configuration.

15
00:00:51.963 --> 00:00:59.902
The application should define which proxy sources it trusts, how proxy credentials are protected, and whether sensitive service traffic may use a

16
00:00:59.902 --> 00:01:00.263
proxy.

17
00:01:00.263 --> 00:01:04.233
Applications that accept user-controlled URLs need a clear destination model.

18
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:06.759
Some features should only call approved services.

19
00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:10.728
Some should accept only preconfigured endpoint names rather than arbitrary URLs.

20
00:01:10.728 --> 00:01:17.223
Where a destination allowlist is used, it should consider scheme, hostname, port, and any service-specific path requirements.

21
00:01:17.223 --> 00:01:19.389
The key habit is bounded behavior.

22
00:01:19.389 --> 00:01:25.884
Redirect counts, allowed schemes, credential forwarding, proxy use, and destination selection should be visible in code and tests.

23
00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:29.853
That makes the client easier to operate and safer to change.

24
00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:37.071
It also prevents configuration drift from becoming invisible policy, because reviewers can compare the intended destination model with what the

25
00:01:37.071 --> 00:01:41.040
running application will actually allow in each environment before release review.
